;; default-watch — a free, no-payment process whose only job is to make an event watch
;; addressable by Sharetribe's email system.
;;
;; WHY A TRANSACTION AT ALL
;;
;; A notification's :to must be :actor.role/customer or :actor.role/provider — the two
;; parties OF THAT TRANSACTION. There is no way to email a third party. So for Sharetribe
;; to email someone who is merely watching an event, the watch itself has to be a
;; transaction and the watcher has to be its customer. That is the whole reason this
;; process exists. Do not "simplify" it into an array on the user's publicData: that would
;; be tidier and would silently break every alert.
;;
;; Side benefit: "who is watching event X" becomes transactions.query({listingId}), a
;; first-class indexed query, instead of paging the whole userbase and filtering in memory.
;;
;;   :transition/watch (customer)
;;         │
;;         ▼
;;   :state/watching ──────┐
;;         │   ▲           │ :transition/ticket-dropped (operator, self-loop)
;;         │   └───────────┘ fires :notification/ticket-dropped to the customer
;;         │
;;         ├── :transition/unwatch (customer)
;;         └── :transition/operator-unwatch (operator)
;;                   │
;;                   ▼
;;             :state/unwatched
;;
;; THE 100-TRANSITION CEILING — READ BEFORE CHANGING THE FAN-OUT
;;
;; Sharetribe caps a transaction at 100 transitions total. Because ticket-dropped loops
;; back into :state/watching, a watch on a busy event burns one transition per alert and
;; dies silently at the ceiling — no error, the watcher simply stops hearing anything.
;; The fan-out MUST check transaction.attributes.transitions.length and, past a threshold
;; (~90), stop transitioning that watch and re-create it instead. See
;; server/api-util/watcherAlerts.js.
;;
;; The listing on this transaction is the EVENT, not the dropped ticket. The ticket's
;; asking price, face value and URL are not otherwise reachable from here, so
;; ticket-dropped carries them in as protected data and the email template reads them
;; back out. Without update-protected-data the email cannot show the price comparison,
;; which is the entire point of it.

{:format :v3,
 :transitions
 [{:name :transition/watch,
   :actor :actor.role/customer,
   :actions [],
   :to :state/watching}

  ;; Operator-only, because the Integration API can invoke operator transitions and
  ;; nothing else. Self-loop: the watch stays live so it can alert again.
  {:name :transition/ticket-dropped,
   :actor :actor.role/operator,
   :actions [{:name :action/update-protected-data}],
   :from :state/watching,
   :to :state/watching}

  {:name :transition/unwatch,
   :actor :actor.role/customer,
   :actions [],
   :from :state/watching,
   :to :state/unwatched}

  ;; Needed for account deletion. server/api/delete-account.js runs as operator and
  ;; cannot invoke the customer's own unwatch, so it needs this one to retire the
  ;; watches of a user who is leaving.
  {:name :transition/operator-unwatch,
   :actor :actor.role/operator,
   :actions [],
   :from :state/watching,
   :to :state/unwatched}],

 :notifications
 [{:name :notification/ticket-dropped,
   :on :transition/ticket-dropped,
   :to :actor.role/customer,
   :template :watch-ticket-dropped}]}
